{%- comment -%}
  "How was your experience?" — an experience rating on a delivered order.
  Usage: {% render 'order-experience-rating', order: order %}

  Judge.me holds the rating; this is the prompt, not the store. A theme has no
  database and the storefront cannot write to Shopify — an Admin token is the
  only thing that can put a value on an order, and a token in theme JS is the
  whole shop handed to anyone who opens View Source. So the stars hand off to
  Judge.me's shop-review form, which already collects store ratings, already
  keeps them against the order, and already sends the request on its own.

  That hand-off is the point: Judge.me's automated request goes out after
  fulfilment whether or not anyone returns to this page. This is for the
  customer who comes back to check an order and is willing to say something
  while they are here.

  Two gates, both deliberate.

  Fulfilment. An order placed an hour ago has nothing to rate. The customer
  has seen a checkout form — the packing, the courier, whether COD went
  smoothly at the door, none of it has happened yet. Asking then measures the
  checkout and files the answer under service. So the card waits for the order
  to actually be fulfilled.

  A set URL. With no form to open, these would be five dead stars on a real
  order. Blank setting, nothing renders — the same fallback the WhatsApp float
  makes rather than linking nowhere.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign form_url = settings.judgeme_shop_review_url | strip
  assign is_fulfilled = false
  if order.fulfillment_status == 'fulfilled'
    assign is_fulfilled = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if form_url != blank and is_fulfilled -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The chosen rating rides along as a query parameter, so clicking the third
    star opens the form already showing three. Judge.me decides whether to
    honour it; if it does not, the form opens unset and the customer picks
    there — the same page either way, so this can only help. The separator is
    worked out rather than assumed: the link a merchant pastes out of Judge.me
    usually carries a ?shop_domain= already, and a second ? would break it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if form_url contains '?' -%}
    {%- assign sep = '&' -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- assign sep = '?' -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  <div class="co-block">
    <div class="co-step">
      <span class="n"><span class="mi" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:18px">star</span></span>
      <h2>How was your experience?</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="oer-sub">Order {{ order.name }} has been delivered. Tell us how it went — the packaging, the delivery, the scent itself. It takes a few seconds, and it is what the next person shopping reads.</p>
    {%- comment -%}
      Five links rather than a widget: each one is a real destination, so this
      works with no JavaScript and a screen reader is told what each star does
      instead of meeting five identical icons.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="oer-stars" role="group" aria-label="Rate your experience with order {{ order.name }}">
      {%- for i in (1..5) -%}
        <a class="oer-star" href="{{ form_url }}{{ sep }}rating={{ i }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
           aria-label="Rate {{ i }} out of 5 and open the review form">
          <span class="mi mi-f" aria-hidden="true">star</span>
        </a>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
